    "result_text": "All tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS). Here's what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: on `POST /api/owners`, when no `registrationDate` is supplied, default it to the server's current date, and always return `registrationDate` in ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` form (preserving a supplied date otherwise).\n\nChanges, kept small and cohesive to minimize structural impact:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an optional `registrationDate` field (`type: string, format: date`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. Because `Owner` is `allOf` `OwnerFields`, the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` now accept it on input and return it on output as `LocalDate` (serialized as `YYYY-MM-DD`), and MapStruct maps it automatically by name \u2014 so no controller or mapper edits were needed.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `registrationDate` field + getter/setter, plus one small `@PrePersist` method that defaults it to `LocalDate.now()` only when null. With `IDENTITY` id generation the insert (and thus the callback) fires immediately on save, so both the create response and the later GET see the value. A supplied date flows through untouched.\n\n3. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** and **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 added the `registration_date DATE` column (the test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema is authoritative) and padded the positional seed inserts with a null value. I followed the existing convention where only the hsqldb schema is kept in sync with the entity (the h2/mysql/postgres schemas already omit `email`; h2, the default runtime profile, uses `ddl-auto=update` and self-adds the column).\n\nThe default logic lives in one tiny, low-complexity method inside the entity rather than being threaded through the large controller or mapper.",
